#{[#edb/ref 17592186054389 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(\\land)$ and \"or\" $(\\lor)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A \\land B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is false, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q \\lor R$ is true, but the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{false}∧\\overset{\\text{true}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∨\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186054398 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, consider the expression inside the parenthesis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2} & = \\\\ 5÷4 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{\\frac{5}{4}}{\\overset{⏞}{5÷(-2)^{2}}})\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{5\\times 4}{4\\times 5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{\\cancel{5}\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times \\cancel{5}} & = \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186055061 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is true. Therefore, $P∧Q$ is true, and the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∧Q)∧R & ≡\\overset{\\text{true}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∧\\text{true})}}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∧\\text{true} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186055088 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $R$ is true, $S$ is true, and $T$ is false. Therefore, $S∧T$ is false, but the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R∨(S∧T) & ≡\\text{true}∨\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{true}∧\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186064081 "Let $u = x^2+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\cdot x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\overset{u-1}{\\overset{⏞}{{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\overset{\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u}{\\overset{⏞}{x\\,\\text{d}x}} \\\\  & =\\int (u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (u-1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({x}^{2}+1)^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186106485 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c478dd4988aaf23348c2453e1f9fca137ddd33284cde7cb7eae1775982bd4b2e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)+f(1)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.5$ | $3$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.5+3+2.5+2)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =11\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.75\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186106541 "In this case, the expression for $w(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 2{sech}^{2}⁡2x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\overset{g(x)}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x)}}}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x.$" #edb/ref 13194139535541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186119603 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186119604 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186119605 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186119607 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_2E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186120182 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=-1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-3.$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186120183 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186120186 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186120187 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -16 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186120188 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186120190 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186120193 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 10 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=5,{u}_{33}=-6,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-30.$" #edb/ref 13194139536011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186120519 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186133512 "To work out $\\left(-13\\right)^3,$ we multiply $-13$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-13$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-13)^{3} & =\\overset{169}{\\overset{⏞}{(-13)\\cdot (-13)}}\\cdot (-13) \\\\  & =169\\cdot (-13) \\\\  & =-2\\,197\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186133514 "To work out $\\left(-0.5\\right)^3,$ we multiply $-0.5$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-0.5$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-0.5)^{3} & =\\overset{0.25}{\\overset{⏞}{(-0.5)\\cdot (-0.5)}}\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & =0.25\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & =-0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186133612 "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I,$ and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=28 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=14.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=7.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3(7) & =28 \\\\ 7{x}_{1} & =49 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/c3/c3f08e2cdc175b34030857c03d0301e5b734975355d09b2016559b4f28b6f5e4.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186133613 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6.75 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y = 6.75 - x.$" #edb/ref 13194139535471 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186133614 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 \\\\ 1.8 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 4.2\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.04 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y = 0.04+ 0.82x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the spring will stretch if an $8$-ounce mass is hung from it,  we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.04+0.82(8) \\\\  & =6.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, according to the model, if an $8$ ounce mass is hung on the spring, the spring will stretch $6.6$ inches. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 13194139535471 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186133615 "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{b