\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is attained when $\\mathbf{x}={\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nIn this case, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ and the column space.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}-\\mathbf{b}\\Vert =\\sqrt{(-12)^2+0^2+(-12)^2+0^2}=12\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535471 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186133718 "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}-6$** | **$\\color{blue}-3$** | **$\\color{blue}-1$** | **$\\color{blue}0$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}-7$ | $\\color{red}-4$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139535471 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134106 "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139535471 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134108 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1.7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6-1.7x.$" #edb/ref 13194139535471 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134111 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134114 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$8$** | **$16$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-10$** | **$-8$** | **$-5$** | **$-1$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $10$ | $16$ | $22$ | $30$ |\n\n \n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 16 & 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 22 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139536171 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134324 "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{12}{6})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{6})=2\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134326 "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{9}{9})\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134329 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134330 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536171 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134335 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 5{x}_{2} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+7(1) & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134352 "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=2{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-8{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=24{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & 24 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-8+4+(-6)=-10.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134354 "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{6}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-2{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-4{v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=8{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=4{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=16{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+16+6=26.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134356 "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 0=0.$" #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134362 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8.3 \\\\ 5.1 \\\\ 10.9 \\\\ 10.7 \\\\ 9.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44.5 \\\\ 141.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6.5 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6.5+0.8x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6.5+0.8(6) \\\\  & =11.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$11\\,300.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134363 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 72 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 72 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134364 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -7 \\\\ -7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134366 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134387 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 25 \\\\ 3 & 32 \\\\ 4 & 42 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139536171 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134389 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139536171 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134390 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186134392 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139535472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186147187 "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4$ with multiplication signs:\n$\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^{\\color{blue}{4}} = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8}}_{\\large { { {\\color{blue}4}} \\text{ times}}}$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{3}{8})^{4}|= \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}; = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8}; = (3 \\cdot 3^(⏞)^(9) \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3^(⏞)^(9))/(8 \\cdot 8_(⏟)_(64) \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8_(⏟)_(64)); = \\frac{9 \\cdot 9}{64 \\cdot 64}; = \\frac{81}{4096}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{81}{4\\,096}}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186147932 "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n{{(6-2)}_{⏟}}_{4}+{2}^{3}=4+{2}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n4\\,+\\overset{8}{\\overset{⏞}{{2}^{3}}}=4+8\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n4+8=12\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186152481 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 2000, and November 1st, 2000. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 4th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to November 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(3)+29(1)}}+3.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,29≡1,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(29)+3 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(1)+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡14\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as January 1st, 2000, namely Saturday." #edb/ref 13194139533599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157226 "We can write down the number using a place value chart:\n\n| **Place Values** | $8^1$ | $8^0$ | . | $8^{-1}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $5$ | $6$ | . | $6$ |\n\nTherefore, our number can be written in base $10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(56.6)_{8} &= 5 \\cdot 8^{1} + 6 \\cdot 8^{0}^(⏞)^(whole) + 6 \\cdot 8^{-1}^(⏞)^(fraction) \\\\\n&= 40 + 6 + 0.75 \\\\\n&= (46.75)_{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157228 "We can write down the number using a place value chart:\n\n| **Place Values** | $7^0$ | . | $7^{-1}$ | $7^{-2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $4$ | . | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, our number can be written in base $10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4.31)_{7} &= 4 \\cdot 7^{0}^(⏞)^(whole) + 3 \\cdot 7^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 7^{-2}^(⏞)^(fraction) \\\\\n&= 4 + \\frac{3}{7} + \\frac{1}{49} \\\\\n&= \\frac{218}{49}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157673 "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overset{\\text{Always true}}{\\overset{⏞}{Q}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, we proceed as follows:\n\nThe theorem can be written as the implication \n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,|n|>-1.\n$$\n\nNow, since $|n|$ is non-negative for all $n\\in Z,$ we have the following fact:\n\nThe consequent $|n|>-1$ of our implication is always true.\n\nFinally, we can make the following conclusion:\n\nTherefore, the theorem is trivially true." #edb/ref 13194139533458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157779 "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overset{\\text{Always true}}{\\overset{⏞}{Q}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always true. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186157781 "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{⏞}{P}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186161899 "We can write down the binary fraction using a place value chart:\n\n| **Place Values** | $2^1$ | $2^0$ | . | $2^{-1}$ | $2^{-2}$ | $2^{-3}$ | $2^{-4}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $1$ | $1$ | . | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, our number in base $10$ can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(10.0101)_{2} &= 1 \\cdot 2^{1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{0}^(⏞)^(whole) + 1 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2} + 0 \\cdot 2^{-3} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-4}^(⏞)^(fraction) \\\\\n&= 2 + 1 + 0.5 + 0.25 + 0 + 0.0625 \\\\\n&= (3.8125)_{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186161900 "We express the number $(3.25)_{10}$ in base $10$ as follows:\n\n- For the whole number part ($3$), we extract the powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one.\n- For the fractional part, we extract the *negative* powers of $2$ from the number, starting from the largest possible one.\n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.25 &= 3^(⏞)^(whole) + 0.25^(⏞)^(fraction) \\\\\n&= 2^{1} + 2^{0} + 2^{-2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2^{1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{0}_(⏟)_(whole) + 0 \\cdot 2^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 2^{-2}_(⏟)_(fraction)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding place value chart is as follows:\n\n| **Place Values** | $2^{1}$ | $2^{0}$ | . | $2^{-1}$ | $2^{-2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Digits** | $1$ | $1$ | . | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(3.25)_{10} = (11.01)_2\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186162413 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}\\times \\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/04/045903aeef84fbd38d09e97f203d9169f0c1f10b662e343b82fc8113dbd1ecc3.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/81/8107e3dbe0cd180f8ce4ac8760f3fb341cb6d4accf1c698856aec361d4db23b7.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,9).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(10,6)$ and $(8,9).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,6).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8<X\\le 10,6<Y\\le 9) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(10,9)-F(10,6)-F(8,9)+F(8,6) \\\\  & =0.72-0.3-0.15+0.08 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186162415 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/77/7790a013e37bb86c00b44b1c022d6597f9bbdd57616c48e7ee7dfe8ddf512c63.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/37/379f0690fdbe92b7fb8f66cce4f12b214a23c6299fb8df71edf56d37fc0387fe.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(9,10)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,10)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/90/90a6730747b59a9886acbdf21880ca6a7ec3266142522ea5b32f07090963a98d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X\\le 9,Y\\le 10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(9,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 9,Y\\le 10)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 10)}} \\\\  & =F(9,10)-F(3,10) \\\\  & =0.65-0.12 \\\\  & =0.53.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186162421 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/9e/9ebff161899c6f61af3dffc1a5a0069de14bafd9b138293a945138e2951d066d.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(7,1)$ and bottom-left corner at $(3,-2)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/e2/e25278ed6d9712d8d2d2cabda5d1d0dbbf10aa8193f1342e7b4b66b26f9154e3.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(7,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(7,-2)$ and $(3,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,-2).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\le X\\le 8,-1\\le Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(7,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{bottom-right corner at }(7,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 7,Y\\le -2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-left corner at }(3,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{bottom-left corner at }(3,-2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le -2)}} \\\\  & =F(7,1)-F(7,-2)-F(3,1)+F(3,-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171778 "Recall that a random variable's probability distribution is uniquely determined by its moment-generating function (MGF). Therefore, if the MGF of a random variable $X$ matches that of a known distribution, then $X$ has the same distribution.\n\nFor a binomial random variable $Y_i \\sim \\textrm{B}(n,p)$, we have\n\n$$\nM_{Y_i}(t) = (1-p+pe^t)^n\n$$\n\nIn our case, since $Y_i \\sim \\textrm{B}(5,0.3)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{Y_{i}}(t) &= (1 - 0.3 + 0.3e^{t})^{5} \\\\\n&= (0.7 + 0.3e^{t})^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\nY = Y_1+Y_2+\\cdots + Y_{10}\n$$\n\nwhere $Y_1, Y_2,\\ldots Y_{10}$ are independent random variables with moment-generating function $M_{Y_i}(t)$, then the moment-generating function of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nM_Y(t) = M_{Y_1}(t) \\cdot M_{Y_2}(t) \\cdots M_{Y_{10}}(t)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{Y}(t) &= (0.7 + 0.3e^{t})^{5} \\cdot (0.7 + 0.3e^{t})^{5}⋯(0.7 + 0.3e^{t})^{5}^(⏞)^(10times) \\\\\n&= (0.7 + 0.3e^{t})^{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $M_Y(t)$ is the MGF of a binomial random variable with $n = 50$ and $p= 0.3$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $Y\\sim B(50,0.3)$." #edb/ref 13194139533371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186171900 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v \\\\ u-5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v+1 \\\\ u-5v\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 3u - v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{1}, \\qquad y = u + (\\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{-5})v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{0} \\, .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+(-5)+0 = -4.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535473 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186172295 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u+4v \\\\ 2u-5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u+4v+2 \\\\ 2u-5v+1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-u+4v+2,\\,y=2u-5v+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+2+1=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535473 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186172297 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3u+4v \\\\ -5u+3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3u+4v \\\\ -5u+3v\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-3u+4v,\\,y=-5u+3v+0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+3+0=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535473 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186172337 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=80.6+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=80.6+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>4.303)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.025}=4.303.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(3-6)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(7-6)^{2}+(9-6)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=4.303\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.35}{20}}≈0.6\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=5\\pm 0.6\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186172372 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-4-0.1x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=4-0.1x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.1.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(24-28)^{2}+(27-28)^{2}+(30-28)^{2}+(31-28)^{2} \\\\  & =16+1+4+9 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.007}{30}}≈0.04\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.1\\pm 0.04\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186175860 "Recall that $F(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/18/181df1b4d320fac30e8fc774975c8e1ae3fa6f4d360a49a38d23f1de96ebf7cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,5).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $\\color{red}(5,2)$ and ${\\color{red}{(0,5)}}.$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ${\\color{blue}{(0,2)}}.$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 5,2\\le Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,5)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,2)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,5)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,2)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 2)}} \\\\  & =F(5,5)-F(5,2)-F(0,5)+F(0,2) \\\\  & =0.9-0.3-0.45+0.15 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186176357 "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/a3/a3f2d39dd5b14494c726d5bc758fb9d89c5cdc18831fc900cae68da4cf5b6dcf.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,9)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,5)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/fa/fa3c9972c42d877a17609ead70776226ab4effccc991ecece596f542efea8cad.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10,5\\le Y\\le 9) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 9)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & =F(10,9)-F(10,5) \\\\  & =0.7-0.2 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186179592 "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k+1)! = (k+1) \\cdot k \\cdot (k-1) \\cdot (k-2) \\cdot \\: \\cdots \\: \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)! & =(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\overset{(k-1)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot (k-1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186179998 "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k+2)!=(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+2)! & =(k+2)\\cdot \\overset{(k+1)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186192979 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.5+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=3.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.101)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{18,0.025}=2.101.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.101\\cdot \\sqrt{8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{24})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.5\\pm 2.8\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186198076 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y) = (0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186198645 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =4{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+4\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+x+(-2)y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-2)+(-2)+2=-2.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186198655 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+(-4)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{2}+2xy+(-2){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-\\frac{1}{2})+2+(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186203675 "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $3-\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rearrange the equation, leaving a root on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $3 - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b} - 3; \\sqrt{2}|= 3 - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{3b}{b} - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{3b - a}{b}^(⏞)^(c)$\n\nNow, if we define $c=3b-a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=3b-a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational." #edb/ref 13194139533378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186203724 "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nis rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the equation to give a radical on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (2a^(⏞)^(c))/(b)$.\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=2a$. Since $2$ and $a$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is irrational." #edb/ref 13194139533378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186233344 "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n-4)!=(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)\\cdot (n-6)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-4)! & =(n-4)\\cdot \\overset{(n-5)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-5)\\cdot (n-6)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186233345 "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n-2)!=(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot (n-4)\\cdot (n-5)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-2)! & =(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot \\overset{(n-4)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot (n-4)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186233346 "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n+3)!=(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+3)! & =(n+3)\\cdot \\overset{(n+2)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186233347 "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n+6)!=(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+6)! & =(n+6)\\cdot \\overset{(n+5)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186233551 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/41/41fde96921443be6c1efc28800e921a189fbe32d8a43667f6a85e7cf399eb745.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\approx (\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $0.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx (0.75+1+0.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186235418 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/e1/e13444351bb092ced6fc788ae921622f154a267e9544e928700736a53a259fc6.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.75$ | $3$ | $1.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.75+3+1.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =8.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.25\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186235475 "Since $x\\ge 3,$ we have that $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nFirst, let's compute the step size $Δx,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our integral are $a=4$ and $b=7,$ and there are $n=3$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{7-4}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/18/18d2d2d79746d5782d27769b644b31f36c76fc7cf9d867788490d74935439b4e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(4)+f(5)+f(6)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n| $5$ | $15$ |\n| $6$ | $24$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(8+15+24)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186243945 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243947 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243948 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 15 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-5,{u}_{22}=6,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-30.$" #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243949 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243950 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 14 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243951 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$" #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243952 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=3.$" #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243953 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243964 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243967 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=2,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=8.$" #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243968 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{2}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-2,$ ${u}_{33}=3,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-6.$" #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186243969 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{2}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,$ ${u}_{33}=4,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-4.$" #edb/ref 13194139535475 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186250475 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nLet's set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $22$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+16 & =22 \\\\ 2k & =22-16 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186250476 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(0)+f(3)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nLet's set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(5+(-2)+k)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(k+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3k+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $6$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3k+9 & =6 \\\\ 3k & =6-9 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186253254 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/8f/8fe84e0056eaa8320b4824740a259226b1a18ff1b64ba648d8740d6cac8703fc.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)+f(5)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+3}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{3+7}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2+15 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186253255 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/25/25dc419eb5711b0d990909c611a6eacb2754285fb45dc0d969207e63dc09ba12.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(-1)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-1)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{2+4}{2}\\cdot (-1-(-3))+\\frac{4+8}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{8+6}{2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+6+14 \\\\  & =26\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186259639 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ -9u+4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+3 \\\\ -9u+4v+4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=1u+0v+3,\\,y=-9u+4v+4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+0+4=5.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535476 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186259640 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+8v \\\\ u\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+8v-2 \\\\ u+5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=6u+8v-2,\\,y=u+5\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=6+8+5=19.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535476 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186259641 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2u+v \\\\ 9u+3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2u+v \\\\ 9u+3v\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-2u+v,\\,y=9u+3v\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-2+9+3=10.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535476 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186259642 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u-v \\\\ 9u+5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u-v-2 \\\\ 9u+5v-3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=4u+(-1)v-2,\\,y=9u+5v-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-1+9+5=13.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535476 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186259643 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+6v \\\\ 3u-4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+6v \\\\ 3u-4v\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=1u+6v+0,\\,y=3u-4v\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+0+3=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535476 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186259644 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6u-2v \\\\ 4u+7v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6u-2v+1 \\\\ 4u+7v-2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-6u-2v+1,\\,y=4u+7v-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-6+1+7=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535476 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186269247 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $6$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139535478 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186269255 "| $x$ | **$-5$** | **$0$** | **$5$** | **$10$** | **$15$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $10$ | $5$ | $0$ | $-5$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139535478 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186269256 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $5$ | $8$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139535478 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186271956 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=2+1.4x.$" #edb/ref 13194139535478 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186271957 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation \n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7.5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=7.5+3x.$" #edb/ref 13194139535478 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186271958 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{56}\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6.25 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$