\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535493 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186402054 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot D \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535493 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186402736 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.4 \\\\ 1 & 2.8 \\\\ 1 & 3.2 \\\\ 1 & 3.6\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 88\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-1+2.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the amount of the company's weekly operating expenses if the telephone is used for a total of $4$ hours, we substitute $x=4$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+2.5(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the company's weekly operating expenses will be $$900.$ \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=4$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 13194139535493 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186402743 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10.8 \\\\ 1.6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=10.8+1.6x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10.8+1.6(6) \\\\  & =20.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$20\\,400.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 13194139535493 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186402789 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 25 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 32 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 42 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139535493 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186403815 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535493 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186407132 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $k$ is *bound* since it's placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n∀{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,n+{k}^{2}\\ge k\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $n$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $n.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $k$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∀k$ that uses $k:$\n\n$$\n∀k\\in (0,1),\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∀k}{\\overset{⏞}{n+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}}\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n∀\\,{k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in (0,1),\\,{n}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}+{k}^{2}\\ge {k}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186407134 "Let's examine our expression and mark the occurrences of the variables as bound $(\\text{b})$ or free $(\\text{f}).$\n\n- Occurrence I of the variable $x$ is *bound* since it is placed right next to the quantifier $∀:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,)\n$$\n\n- Occurrence II of the variable $x$ is *free* since it isn't placed right next to a quantifier and it doesn't lie within the scope of a quantifier that uses $x.$\n\n- Occurrence III of the variable $y$ is *bound* since it lies within the scope of the quantifier $∃y$ that uses $y:$\n\n$$\n(\\,∀x\\in R,\\,0<x<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃y\\in R,\\,\\overset{\\text{scope of }∃y}{\\overset{⏞}{x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}}})\n$$\n\nThe occurrences of variables in the expression are as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\,∀{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,0<{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}<1\\,)⇒(\\,∃{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\in R,\\,{x}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{f}\\end{aligned}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}_{\\begin{aligned} \\\\ ↑ \\\\ \\text{b}\\end{aligned}}\\,)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the free occurrence is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 13194139533530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186410713 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1600, and October 1st, 1600. \n\n- There were $5$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, and August).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and October 6th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to October 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(5)+30(3)+29(1)}}+5.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,29≡1,\\,5≡5,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5(31)+3(30)+1(29)+5 \\\\  & ≡5(3)+3(2)+(1)+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡15+6+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1+6+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡13\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date 6 days after January 1st, 1600, namely Friday." #edb/ref 13194139533599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186410716 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1995, and September 1st, 1995. \n\n- There were $5$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July,  and  August).\n\n- There were $2$ months with $30$ days (April and  June).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and September 19th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to September 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(5)+30(2)+28(1)}}+18.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,18≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5(31)+2(30)+1(28)+18 \\\\  & ≡5(3)+2(2)+1(0)+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡15+4+0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1+4+0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $2$ days after January 1st, 1995, namely Tuesday." #edb/ref 13194139533599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186410718 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1700, and November 1st, 1700. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 16th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to November 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(3)+28(1)}}+15.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,15≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(28)+15 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(0)+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $4$ days after January 1st, 1700, namely Tuesday." #edb/ref 13194139533599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186410723 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 2100, and December 1st, 2100. \n\n- There are $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There are $4$ months with $30$ days (April, June, September, and November).\n\n- There is $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and December 25th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to December 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(4)+28(1)}}+24.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,24≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+4(30)+1(28)+24 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+4(2)+(0)+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+8+0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+1+0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $1$ day after January 1st, 2100, namely Saturday." #edb/ref 13194139533599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186410810 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}{x}_{2} &=-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}(0) & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14}{x}_{1} & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186410811 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} &=38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=-10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}(-5) & =38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186415504 "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{11}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=-2.$" #edb/ref 13194139535494 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186415505 "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} & \\frac{4}{9} & \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=1.$" #edb/ref 13194139535494 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186415506 "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-2{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=4{q}_{1}-8{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{12}+{r}_{33}=3+(-2)+4=5.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186432326 "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-30{q}_{1}+5{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=8{q}_{1}-4{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -30 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}=3+8+(-4)=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186432329 "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{9}{9})\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=2\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535495 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186432330 "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=1.$" #edb/ref 13194139535495 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186432334 "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{1}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &={v}_{1}+{v}_{2}={q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=-{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=-{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=1+(-1)+3=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186432370 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y+1}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y+1}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x+y+1)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot xy+(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+1x+1y-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2})xy-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186432374 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+y+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{2}{(x+y+1)^{3}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+(-1)\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-1)x-y+{x}^{2}+2xy+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-1+2+1=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186435170 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u \\\\ 6u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u \\\\ 6u+2v-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=4u+0,\\,y=6u+2v+(-1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+2+(-1)=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535496 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186441352 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=4,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=4.$" #edb/ref 13194139535496 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186441353 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 13194139535496 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186441354 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 13194139535496 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186441355 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains numbers different from $1.$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 13194139535496 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186441356 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=5,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=5.$" #edb/ref 13194139535496 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186442810 "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{3})=\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535496 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186475917 "To work out ${(-40)}^{3},$ we multiply $-40$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-40$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-40)^{3} & =\\overset{1\\,600}{\\overset{⏞}{(-40)\\cdot (-40)}}\\cdot (-40) \\\\  & =1\\,600\\cdot (-40) \\\\  & =-64\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186475936 "To work out ${(-20)}^{3},$ we multiply $-20$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-20$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-20)^{3} & =\\overset{400}{\\overset{⏞}{(-20)\\cdot (-20)}}\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =400\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =-8\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186475957 "To work out ${(-9)}^{3},$ we multiply $-9$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-9$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)^{3} & =\\overset{81}{\\overset{⏞}{(-9)\\cdot (-9)}}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =81\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =-729\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186475958 "To work out $(-1.6)^{3},$ we multiply $-1.6$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-1.6$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1.6)^{3} & =\\overset{2.56}{\\overset{⏞}{(-1.6)\\cdot (-1.6)}}\\cdot (-1.6) \\\\  & =2.56\\cdot (-1.6) \\\\  & =-4.096\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186475968 "To work out $(0.01)^{3},$ we multiply $0.01$ by itself and then multiply the result by $0.01$ once more:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0.01}^{3} & =\\overset{0.000\\,1}{\\overset{⏞}{0.01\\cdot 0.01}}\\cdot 0.01 \\\\  & =0.000\\,1\\cdot 0.01 \\\\  & =0.000\\,001\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186475977 "To work out ${(-4)}^{3},$ we multiply $-4$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-4$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{3} & =\\overset{16}{\\overset{⏞}{(-4)\\cdot (-4)}}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-64\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186478379 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$" #edb/ref 13194139535497 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186478385 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{1}{2},{u}_{33}=-2,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=1.$" #edb/ref 13194139535497 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186478389 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=-5,{u}_{22}=4,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-20.$" #edb/ref 13194139535497 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186478391 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=12.$" #edb/ref 13194139535497 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186478398 "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $(1+{2}^{3}).$\n\n To evaluate $(1+{2}^{3})$ we should also apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n1\\,+\\overset{8}{\\overset{⏞}{{2}^{3}}}=1+8\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n1+8=9\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n5\\times {{(1+{2}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{9}=5\\times 9\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we perform the **M**ultiplication:\n\n$$\n5\\times 9=45\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186478403 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535497 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186478404 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=-\\frac{1}{3},{u}_{33}=2,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535497 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186478418 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 9 & -15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$" #edb/ref 13194139535497 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186478422 "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $({5}^{2}-1).$\n\n To evaluate $({5}^{2}-1)$ we should also apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overset{25}{\\overset{⏞}{{5}^{2}}}-1=25-1\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **S**ubtraction:\n\n$$\n25-1=24\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n{{({5}^{2}-1)}_{⏟}}_{24}\\,÷8=24÷\\,8\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we perform the **D**ivision:\n\n$$\n24÷8=3\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186478426 "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n{{(1+5)}_{⏟}}_{6}{}^{2}+{4}^{3}={6}^{2}+{4}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overset{36}{\\overset{⏞}{{6}^{2}}}+\\overset{64}{\\overset{⏞}{{4}^{3}}}=36+64\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n36+64=100\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186478435 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{2}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535497 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186478437 "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n{{(2+3)}_{⏟}}_{5}-{2}^{2}=5-{2}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n5\\,-\\overset{4}{\\overset{⏞}{{2}^{2}}}=5\\,-4\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **S**ubtraction:\n\n$$\n5-4=1\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186481341 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,6,10\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/00/0036b6bd64900e2c6a0319f16788b2d563a8415256e2f21ba561b1fb41afdf34.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ and top-left corner at $(6,6),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/43/4394f404e54b8e585becb601f688eece3d4769efe2a124cb53b11d3432a9996b.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,6)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(6,6)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/5e/5ed86031d5762e3a2fe440fba52775fc6833243664fa18ee85c3449b50ff6cee.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6,Y\\le 8) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 6)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(6,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 6,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & =F(12,6)-F(6,6) \\\\  & =0.7-0.4 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186481349 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/01/01e04dfdac5f4abc16bd856dcfbe1b7361010cce2e7779658a33f293c1c52ae4.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2d/2d2525118ca3b62c0829c0308fab168d0b806dfa13302c007a63cd354314d63f.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(4,-1)$ and $(1,1).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,-1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 4,-1<Y\\le 1) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le -1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,-1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le -1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,1)-F(4,-1)-F(1,1)+F(1,-1) \\\\  & =0.77-0.15-0.19+0.09 \\\\  & =0.52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186481372 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6\\}\\times \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/2b/2b159f4ba1873270f42b7afd2fdc4b969672c7d3cb7e694d519ab82dab33b283.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/e1/e186cf0e38e938798cdcd5bc10a5334230cc78cc77d114a14eba0c07702a1cdc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,3)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/eb/ebcfd8e0b553cab38a76eac77aab45186db92b3fe47a1fbb87a8623db4c29389.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4,1<Y\\le 3) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 3)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(4,3)-F(4,1) \\\\  & =0.8-0.3 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186481393 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/2d/2dd2929b39c9d0357516ebea9f1f31ed2b5f64c6154184ec904996c51162c144.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(5,3)$ and bottom-left corner at $(2,1)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/db/db601c0b0d2931a61d2218e196ea7485fc8d258cb77c604dc44661687de43beb.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,3).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(2,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 5,2\\le Y<4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,3)-F(5,1)-F(2,3)+F(2,1) \\\\  & =0.6-0.23-0.32+0.05 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186481396 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}\\times \\{3,6,9,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/f4/f43c5ae8d27872c4aebe235c507600b7bf1806e22b52dd3b3ff2261509ac9925.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the rectangular region with its top-right corner at $(10,9)$ and bottom-left corner at $(4,3)$ below, where the top and right boundaries are inclusive and the bottom and left boundaries are exclusive.\n\n![](images/60/60bf5a958b682d513010c3eff9a8084072be62e286c0b0dfacb09960a3283e38.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,9).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(10,3)$ and $(4,9).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,3).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<11,4<Y<10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 3)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 9)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,3)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 3)}} \\\\  & =F(10,9)-F(10,3)-F(4,9)+F(4,3) \\\\  & =0.55-0.15-0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186481406 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\times \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/dd/dd49cc4b7cb5cc80bb325819c5c8f3c31f8bc3ed762139c2c3b9567a24db2324.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/34/344fec686907843b4b9375db138592f93be9d0232500fb35dceb90086db503cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has i