443] [#edb/ref 17592186558552 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$10$** | **$8$** | **$7$** | **$3$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$8$** | **$12$** | **$14$** | **$19$** | **$22$** |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ | $-10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 15 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 10 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 14 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 19 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 22\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139536171 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186558577 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$50$** | **$58$** | **$62$** | **$65$** | **$70$** |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $17$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 50 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 58 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 62 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 65 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 70\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." #edb/ref 13194139536171 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186561038 "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k-1)!=(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)\\cdot (k-4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-1)! & =(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot \\overset{(k-3)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k-3)\\cdot (k-4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186564531 "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 13194139536071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186565456 "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is false, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q∨R$ is false, but the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∨(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{true}∨\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{false}∨\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186569965 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186569998 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186570009 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536394 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
